so Dan was supposed to make an episode  this week but he was too busy playing  Skyrim to make an episode and so I was  gonna make one instead but I was too  busy playing Skyrim - so sharpen your  sword and grab your shield and let's  take a look at Skyrim so I'm not usually  big on calling spoiler warning in my  episodes because well I'm kind of a  prick  but I am gonna call spoiler warning here  because the game is fairly new so  warning there will be spoilers big  spoilers because we're going to be  looking at the main quest now the reason  for that even though this is a fairly  open-ended game the main quest defines  the central conflict for the game world  and even if you as a player would rather  be off mining or in Markarth the main  quest should still provide a solid  narrative arc with your character as  protagonist in Skyrim you play a  condemned criminal facing the execution  block the nature of your crime is poorly  defined and you might be innocent but  the Empire isn't too picky about due  process luckily a dragon shows up and  torches the place before they give you a  neck up hair cut  as you escape you team up with either  the lovably racist rebels or the lovely  totalitarian empire this scenario is  inherently hilarious because it means  running to safety with the guy who gave  you a death sentence for loitering  everyone's freaking out about the dragon  because in theory dragons don't exist  they're all supposed to be extinct or  imaginary but imaginary friends don't  burn down villages all in all you escape  the village and run away to the nearby  town of Riverwood where you learn more  about the lovable racists and/or the  lovable totalitarians since the dragon  attack is kind of big news on any day of  the week your new friends send you off  to the regional seat of government white  run which is run by whites the Big  Pimpin Ural balgruuf tells you to run  the news by his court wizard who says  that he can help if you go recover an  artifact that may or may not exist this  is just an in-game setup to expose your  character to his destiny which is sort  of like being exposed to cesium 137  probably fatal but it's a fictional  world so instead you get superpowers or  in this case you get a word of power  that you can't use because they want to  set the actual reveal up in a different  way than just using a funky glowing wall  so you return with the rock just in time  for a dragon to attack the western  watchtower unlike the typical video game  the yarol actually rally some troops  instead of sending you to do the job  alone after you and your dirty ride and  crew killed the dragon you go all  Highlander and devouring soul  mechanically this gives you the currency  you need to buy the ability to use the  word you learned off the wall from the  dungeon with the rock in story is to  explain that you must be dovakin or  dragon born because you have the natural  ability to drain power from dragons and  learn to use their words of power and  the Greybeards have summoned you up to  high Hrothgar at the top of the throat  of the world to check you over and see  if you're the real deal the Greybeards  are a sect of pacifist monks who  dedicate their lives to learning Turkic  words of power in order to become one  with creation most of this is explained  in bits and pieces by a bunch of  different NPC's over the course of a  couple hours of gameplay and actually  this is a good time for an interlude to  talk about pacing a lot of modern games  try to emulate other narrative formats  namely movies in terms of the flow of  information and how much time there is  between plot points games are inherently  slower paced than film because even if  what you're doing is advancing the plot  you tip  we have to do things in real time games  can't compress time without taking  control away from the player so if they  want you to climb a mountain then they  either take control away and do it in a  cutscene or you have to climb a mountain  incidentally Skyrim wants you to climb a  mountain when you finally get up to high  Hrothgar the monks run you through a few  tests to see if you're the real deal and  then after you demonstrate your  savant-like ability to master ancient  arcane techniques in seconds as only the  dovahkiin can they send you off to pick  up some old trinket from a tomb because  I don't know I guess the prophecy says  the dovakin has working legs  I guess climbing a mountain wasn't proof  enough whatevs the pacifist wants you to  go kill peeps that's cool when you get  the horn instead you find a note saying  that you're too late meet me at the  river wood in this just further goes to  prove the Greybeards were probably  hoping you just die and stay out of  their hair since someone else was able  to recover the horn anyway and they are  at the dovakin it's cool though since  the thief turns out to be Delphine a hot  warrior lady conspiracy nut she even has  a panic room built into her in she's got  a theory that the Dragons aren't just  returning they're being resurrected and  she wants you to prove that you're the  dovahkiin by murdering a dragon after it  gets up from the grave sho enough the  dragons being resurrected by the first  dragon from the beginning of the game  that dragon named Aldean taunts you for  a bit man flies off to do some more evil  and you kill the other dragon before he  even got to see sunrise again back at  Delphine's crash shacks she's convinced  that the thaw Lemar behind the dragon  invasion because they're trying to take  over the world and want the Empire to be  too weak trying to split attention  between dragons and the rebels to defend  against an invasion to confirm this she  asked you to sneak into a party at the  foreign embassy in the capital and snoop  around a bit she gets you a ticket to  the party and hooks you up with a mole  who will sneak your stuff in he tells  you to bring only the absolute  essentials for a stealth mission so of  course I packed my robot armor sword  shield a dozen potion Scrolls of mass  paralysis Scrolls of hysteria backup  weapons lock picks and proceeded to  murder every last elf I ran into and  stripped the place clean of valuables  all that you find out that the Thalmor  have no idea what's going on but they  name drop an old friend of Delphine's  from back in Nam fetch him from his  rathole  he leads you to sky to Haven temple  we're all doings walls fills out the big  story Halloween was an ancient badass  who ruled the world with an iron fist  some humans were taught the language of  the Dragons and use it to craft a word  of power to kill dragons called dragon  rend they defeated alduin but the  prophecy said he'd come back someday to  end the world unless the dovakin stops  him that starts a long segment where you  get sent to a lot of different places to  find bits and pieces to assemble this  super weapon of a word graybeard send  you to the top of the mound to speak to  a dragon who sends it to the mages guild  who sends you to their library and who  sends you to their insane colleague who  sends you to an ancient ruins who sends  you back in time yada yada yada the  overall plot doesn't really advance all  that much during this time but there is  a really interesting segment where you  talk with Paarthurnax the dragon at the  top of the mountain and he gives you  some long lectures about the nature of  words and how knowing words and their  meanings changes you he also suggests  some interesting philosophy at the end  of the world and implies that the job of  Dovahkiin and Anduin is to end the world  with their conflicts so that the next  world can sprout from the remains  unfortunately this element doesn't pay  off mechanically in the game since  there's never a big button you can push  that says start the apocalypse in fact  the rest plays out really  straightforward you confront and win on  top of the mountain kick his ass he runs  away you capture his friend and  convinced him to portray and when he  flies you to end one secret hideout  where you run through a portal to the  afterlife recruit the ancient heroes  that invented dragon wren kick and  wounds ask for good return to the land  of the living where some dragons stare  you down then fly off and that's it just  you and the wind on top of a mountain  and that's when I realized how badly I'd  screwed up  Skyrim doesn't have a narrative arc  because real life doesn't have a  narrative arc there's stories and mini  arcs but there's no final victory lap  there's where you start and where you  end and everything else in between  you killed Anduin awesome but the world  is still a messed up place the rest of  the Dragons aren't going to lay down  arms just because you killed their boss  and the whole province is still on the  brink of Civil War  everyone else had their things to get  done and since the world didn't end most  people won't even notice that you saved  it ultimately it's not about narrative  arc because the main quest is just a  running  a goal in a long checklist that  introduces you to new threads new people  in new places  the civil war the mages guild the  Thieves Guild the danger princes the  Dark Brotherhood the forsworn uprising  werewolves vampires brigands dragon  shrines and a million other errands and  tasks in the end Skyrim managed to tell  the kind of story that literature film  never can one that doesn't end also you  get to slam a dragon in the face
 

